      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. They reported that since the taxonomy change on 2026-02-20, Event Nexus search returned many low-relevance hits and duplicates, increasing manual triage time to about 6 hours per day for Incident Ops [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nTo get BlueHarbor comfortable with renewal, the proposed proof plan was a short-term mitigation involving updating index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer that maps new taxonomy terms to historical query tokens for the top 20 saved searches. The plan included:\n\n- Receiving schema registry export and 14 days of query logs from BlueHarbor by 2026-03-19.\n- Starting an A/B test in staging on 2026-03-22, lasting 7-10 business days (5 days tuning + 2 days validation).\n- Validation criteria: top-5 correct hit rate \u2265 80% for prioritized queries, no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n- If validation passes, a 1-day production rollout followed by 72 hours of monitoring.\n- A professional services offer of a 90-day block at $48k fixed price to implement the mapping, runbook documentation, and handover, with a 30% discount if Orchestrator expansion is signed concurrently.\n- A conditional credit clause: if the proof does not meet the agreed top-5 accuracy uplift within 10 business days, BlueHarbor would receive a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against the renewal for the upcoming year.\n\nThis proof plan was designed to demonstrate measurable improvement within 7-10 business days to satisfy BlueHarbor's executive mandate to reduce manual triage by 40% and to support renewal discussions [art_bd3560dfe194, art_0bccc580184e].",
